/**
\defgroup cpuid CPU Detection

Import `simd.x86` for baseline x86 capability queries. The raw `simd::cpuid`
function preserves hardware register results, including unsupported-leaf
behavior. Vendor identity is informational and never admits an instruction
profile.

`simd::observe_x86_capabilities()` observes the current logical CPU and OS vector
state. It checks basic leaf ranges and executes XGETBV only after CPUID reports
both XSAVE and OSXSAVE. `simd::classify_x86_profile` is a separate constexpr
operation for real or synthetic snapshots:

\code{.cpp}
import simd.x86;
auto cpu = simd::observe_x86_capabilities();
auto admission = simd::classify_x86_profile(cpu, simd::x86_profile::avx2);
if (!admission.admitted()) {
  // admission.reason() identifies the first missing CPU or OS requirement.
  // The missing_leaf1_ecx/edx, missing_leaf7_ebx and missing_xcr0 masks
  // retain every missing bit in its architectural register position.
}
\endcode

AVX2 admission covers AVX, AVX2, FMA, BMI2, the compiler-implied SSE/POPCNT
features, and enabled XMM/YMM state. AVX512 additionally requires F16C,
AVX512F/DQ/BW/VL and enabled opmask and ZMM state. These requirements match
`simd_target_profile`; application-supplied instruction flags may require
additional checks. Unavailable leaves, unread XCR0, and invalid profile values
cannot admit a profile.

An installed consumer links `simd::common` for module metadata and `simd::simd`
for runtime definitions. It does not import an ISA module or select a profile
just to call these queries. Linking the archive does not import the omnibus
module or inherit its instruction flags. Classification alone is constexpr.

Query outside hot loops; applications own backend choice and tuning policy.
One observation does not establish a common feature set across heterogeneous
CPUs. Use appropriate affinity or system-wide capability policy before entering
specialized code. This API does not modify floating-point state or dispatch
individual vector operations.
*/
